# Track 4 — E2E round 4: 60 vCPU total budget
#
# Rebalanced after docker stats showed the token-issuer (RS256 signing) pinned
# at its 0.6-vCPU cap on all 4 replicas while validators sat at ~5% and the
# selector/handler had headroom. Budget moved validators -> issuer (2.4 -> 5.7
# vCPU), with the request-handler kept matched so it isn't the next bottleneck.
#
# | Service              | cpus x replicas | subtotal |
# |----------------------|-----------------|----------|
# | Redis x 3            | 0.3 x 3         | 0.9      |
# | request-handler      | 0.6 x 8         | 4.8      |
# | verifier-selector    | 1.0 x 1         | 1.0      |
# | token-issuer         | 0.95 x 6        | 5.7      |
# | snark-verifier       | 1.0 x 45        | 45.0     |
# | token-validator (x5) | 0.5 x 5         | 2.5      |
# | TOTAL                |                 | 59.9     |
#
# issuer (~1260/s) and handler (~1325/s) are balanced; verifiers (45) could do
# ~1890/s, so the token pipeline is still the ceiling but ~2.3x the old ~580/s.
# token-validator 0.5 covers each domain's 60% hot window (~0.34 vCPU needed).
# Manager (c5.4xlarge, 16 vCPU) hosts redis+handler+selector+issuer = ~12.4 vCPU.
#
# Load with:  set -a; source e2e-throughput-round4.env; set +a
REDIS_CPUS=0.3
HANDLER_REPLICAS=8
HANDLER_CPUS=0.6
SELECTOR_CPUS=1.0
ISSUER_REPLICAS=6
ISSUER_CPUS=0.95
SNARK_REPLICAS=45
SNARK_COUNT=45
# Applied manually on each App EC2: docker update --cpus=$VALIDATOR_CPUS ...
VALIDATOR_CPUS=0.5
